WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
Take a look at this terminal window.

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.560
You run an NPM install during a React Native upgrade,

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.640
and everything grinds to a halt with a bold red,

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.360
hunk-failed error.

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.840
That failure is actually an intentional safety mechanism.

6
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.440
Your project relies on patches, temporary edits made to third-party code,

7
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:24.240
and the package manager is refusing to silently apply those old edits to newly updated libraries.

8
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.440
We need to clear those errors to get your build working again.

9
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.320
In this guide, we will audit your existing patches,

10
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.240
delete the ones you no longer need,

11
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.520
and safely recreate the rest for your new libraries.

12
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.560
This comparison matrix breaks down the tools we use.

13
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.520
For standard NPM and Yarn version 1 projects,

14
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.760
patch package remains the default tool.

15
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.760
If you operate a modern Mono repo using PNPM or YarnBerry,

16
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.560
you will use their native patching commands instead.

17
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.680
Rather than viewing this broken installation as a disaster,

18
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.920
treat it as a mandatory checkpoint.

19
00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.400
It is an opportunity to clean up the foundational code base of your app.

20
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.880
The first critical step happens before you modify any code at all.

21
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.440
You need to build a comprehensive inventory

22
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.560
of every patch currently sitting in your project.

23
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.640
This structured checklist table shows exactly what you need to track.

24
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.040
For each patch, list the file name,

25
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.800
the library it modifies,

26
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.120
the original reason the patch was written,

27
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.960
and its current status.

28
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.600
The type of file you patched directly dictates

29
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.680
how difficult this process will be.

30
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.240
Javascript files highlighted here in green

31
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.560
are usually straightforward to migrate.

32
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.960
If you patch native C++ or JSI files marked in red,

33
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.800
those updates are highly volatile and will require more effort.

34
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.960
Take the first broken patch on your list

35
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.960
and head straight to the upstream library's official GitHub repository.

36
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.840
You need to check their change log or issue tracker.

37
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.680
This split screen compares the pseudo code of a local patch on the left

38
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.000
with a GitHub release note on the right.

39
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.920
You are looking to see if the maintainers officially merged a fix

40
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.440
that matches the exact intent of your local code.

41
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.920
If you find that the fix landed upstream,

42
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.120
delete your local dot patch file permanently.

43
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.160
Removing an obsolete file is the highest value outcome of this entire process.

44
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.480
It instantly reduces your technical debt

45
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.840
and lowers your project's long-term maintenance burden.

46
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.640
In many cases, you will find that the upstream library

47
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.120
has not resolved your specific issue.

48
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.320
That means you have to manually recreate the patch.

49
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.240
Do not rely on the exact line numbers from your old patch file.

50
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.840
The upgraded library has likely changed its internal structure,

51
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.440
meaning your original fix will no longer align cleanly.

52
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.320
Read the old diff carefully to deduce what the code was actually trying to achieve.

53
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.880
You need to know if it was a critical bug fix,

54
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.640
a temporary workaround, or a custom feature polyfill.

55
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.840
Once you understand the intent, navigate deep

56
00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.480
into your local node modules directory

57
00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.400
and locate the newly updated version of the target file.

58
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.160
Here in the code editor, we are looking inside a specific file

59
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.560
nested under node modules slash react native camera.

60
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.800
This is where you write the equivalent fix

61
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.720
directly into the raw source code of the upgraded library.

62
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.080
You are adapting your old logic to fit the new architecture

63
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.000
of the updated dependency.

64
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.240
The code might look different, but the outcome needs to be identical.

65
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.480
With the edits made and saved, run your package manager's patching command

66
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.680
in the terminal.

67
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.840
This captures your manual edits and generates a fresh patch file.

68
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.200
This manual recreation safely bridges the gap,

69
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.880
translating your app's custom requirements into the new reality

70
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.960
of your upgraded dependencies.

71
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.520
Here in the file explorer, we can verify that the generation process worked.

72
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.920
Your old .patch file is gone, replaced by a newly generated file

73
00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.640
with an incremented version number in its title.

74
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.520
Run a fresh installation command in your terminal.

75
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.800
You should see that the hunk failed error has been eliminated,

76
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.960
confirming the new patch applies smoothly.

77
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.880
There is one final non-negotiable step to complete.

78
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.280
You must document the exact reason why this custom patch exists.

79
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.760
Keep this documentation close to the code.

80
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.360
You can write a dedicated markdown file inside your patches directory,

81
00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.840
or leave a detailed intent comment directly in your package configuration file.

82
00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.000
If you skip this documentation step,

83
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.200
the next developer to upgrade this app will lack context.

84
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.280
They will likely delete the mysterious patch

85
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.280
and unknowingly reintroduce a critical bug straight into production.

86
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.560
Properly documenting your logic transforms a fragile hack

87
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.080
into a stable, easily understood piece of your project's history.

88
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.560
The reward for this meticulous work is a perfectly clean terminal output

89
00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.680
following a successful dependency installation.

90
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.320
You have successfully audited your technical debt,

91
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.920
discarded obsolete files,

92
00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.120
and migrated your essential patches to the new version.

93
00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.600
Achieving a stable patch-free foundation

94
00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.200
is a major milestone in your react native upgrade process.

95
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.360
The core system is now intact.

96
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.360
With your packages installing cleanly,

97
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.680
the immediate next priority is verifying

98
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.960
that those upgraded third-party libraries are actually compatible

99
00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.880
with react native's new architecture.

